---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/insulation';
import { INSULATION, ATTIC_R_BY_ZONE } from '../lib/hvac';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const targets = [30, 38, 49, 60];
---

<CalcPage
  slug="insulation-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Insulation Calculator: R-Value, Depth & Board Feet"
  description="Free insulation calculator. Enter the area, target R-value, and existing insulation to get the depth to add, volume, and board feet for batts, blown-in, spray foam, or foam board."
  h1="Insulation Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Work out how much insulation to add to reach a target R-value: the depth, volume, and board feet for batts, blown-in
    fiberglass or cellulose, spray foam, and foam board.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate insulation</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Pick a target R-value for the space and your climate zone (see below).</li>
    <li>Estimate what's already there: old fiberglass is roughly R-3 per inch.</li>
    <li>Divide the R-value you need to add by the material's R per inch for the depth to add.</li>
    <li>Multiply by the area for volume, or board feet for spray foam.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: topping up a 1,000 sq ft attic</h2>
  <p>
    The attic has about 3½ inches of old batts (R-11), and the target is R-49. Adding R-38 of blown cellulose at about
    R-3.5 per inch takes <strong>10.9 inches</strong>, or roughly 905 cubic feet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="r-values">How much insulation does an attic need?</h2>
  <p>U.S. Department of Energy and ENERGY STAR recommendations for an uninsulated attic:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Climate zone</th><th>Recommended attic R-value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{ATTIC_R_BY_ZONE.map(([z, r]) => <tr><td>{z}</td><td>{r}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Zone 1 is southern Florida and Hawaii; zones 6–8 are the northern states and Alaska. Most of the country falls in zones 3–5.</p>

  <h2 id="materials">R-value per inch by material</h2>
  <p>Typical values; the product label gives the exact figure.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Material</th><th class="num">R per inch</th>{targets.map((t) => <th class="num">Inches for R-{t}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>{INSULATION.map((m) => <tr><td>{m.label}</td><td class="num">{m.rPerIn}</td>{targets.map((t) => <td class="num">{fmt(t / m.rPerIn, 1)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Adding rigid foam to exterior walls during a re-side? The <a href="/siding-calculator/">siding calculator</a> sizes the siding that goes over it.
    Closing up walls or a ceiling afterward? The <a href="/drywall-calculator/">drywall calculator</a> counts sheets and screws.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How many bags of blown insulation do I need?</h3>
  <p>
    Each brand prints a coverage chart on the bag listing bags per 1,000 sq ft for each R-value, already allowing for
    settling. Use the depth from this calculator to check the result, then follow the chart.
  </p>
  <h3>What is a board foot of spray foam?</h3>
  <p>
    One square foot at one inch thick. Contractors quote spray foam per board foot, so 500 sq ft of wall at 3 inches is
    1,500 board feet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Better insulation shrinks the cooling load; re-check the size of your AC with the
    <a href="/btu-calculator/">BTU calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>R to add = target − existing; depth = R to add ÷ R per inch.</li>
    <li>Volume = area × depth; board feet = area × depth in inches.</li>
    <li>Blown insulation settles; the bag chart accounts for that and takes priority.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
